INT. SPACE ELEVATOR CAR - ORBITAL TETHER - DAY

A decommissioned passenger car, windowless but for one thick porthole. Worn leather seats, six of them, bolted in a ring around a central pole. A dial on the wall reads 31,000 km and drops by the second. The air is stale, the lights half-power.

CAPTAIN IDRIS VAUGHAN (50s, gray crew cut, patched uniform jacket) sits at the control panel, reading tablet data. NOOR HASHEMI (30s, flight engineer, braids pinned up, hands always moving) is on her back under the console, wiring. BARTHOLOMEW "BART" CHUKWU (40s, medic, big man in a small seat) peels the foil off a ration bar. FUMIKO SATO (20s, cadet, rigid in her seat, hands gripped white around the harness) stares at the porthole. SVEN LUNDGREN (60s, scientist, lab coat over thermals, a thermos strapped to his belt) hums at the wall.

IDRIS
Noor, status.

NOOR
(from under the console)
Power's holding. Descent's nominal.

IDRIS
Nominal.

NOOR
I said nominal. Didn't say good.

BART
Is there such a thing as a bad descent when you're only falling thirty thousand kilometers?

NOOR
(dry)
Yes, Bart. The kind where you land.

BART
Funny.

FUMIKO
Captain, how long?

IDRIS
Four hours, Cadet.

FUMIKO
Four hours is a long time.

IDRIS
It's the normal time.

SVEN
It is a good time. Time enough for a story.

BART
No stories, Sven.

SVEN
It is a short one.

BART
It's never short.

SVEN
It is the one about the cable.

Noor slides out from under the console. She sits up, a coil of wire in her fist. Idris sets down his tablet. Fumiko turns her eyes from the porthole for the first time.

IDRIS
Not that one, Sven.

SVEN
They should hear it, Captain.

IDRIS
They have clearance to know what they need.

SVEN
Then they need to know.

Silence. The dial reads 29,000. The car shudders, a small tremor along the line. Everyone feels it. Nobody mentions it.

NOOR
Captain, I'd like to hear the story.

IDRIS
No.

NOOR
Captain--

IDRIS
Engineer, return to the panel.

Noor does not move. Bart pops the foil wrapper off his ration bar and eats a bite without chewing.

BART
(to Fumiko, quietly)
Look out the window, kid. Look at the stars.

FUMIKO
They're not moving.

BART
Good.

FUMIKO
They should be.

Bart stops chewing. He looks at Idris. Idris, with great deliberateness, puts the tablet face-down on the panel.

SVEN
They are not moving because we are not descending, Fumiko.

NOOR
That's not funny, Sven.

SVEN
It is not a joke.

IDRIS
Sven, I said no.

SVEN
It is not a thing you can say no to, Captain. The dial is reading. The dial is wrong. We have been stationary since the first tremor, which was ninety minutes ago.

BART
The dial's dropping.

SVEN
The dial is a recording. I looped it myself.

Noor drops the coil of wire. Bart rises, bumping his head on the low ceiling. Fumiko lifts her hands off the harness, finally, and puts them in her lap.

NOOR
You looped it.

SVEN
At the Captain's instruction.

All eyes shift to Idris. His face is perfectly composed. He lays both hands flat on the panel.

IDRIS
There is a fault in the tether.

BART
How bad?

IDRIS
We are holding at thirty thousand and nine hundred kilometers, on a line rated for twenty-two.

NOOR
That's not possible.

IDRIS
It is happening.

FUMIKO
Why didn't you tell us?

IDRIS
Because if I had, you would have done what you're doing now.

FUMIKO
What am I doing?

IDRIS
Looking at me.

Silence. The car groans. Somewhere below them, in the dark, a long cable sings a very high note.

SVEN
(softly)
Now will you let me tell the story?

Idris nods, once. Sven sits, unscrews his thermos, pours a single cup of tea, and holds it out to Fumiko, whose hand reaches for it, and closes, and does not shake.
